## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of modern electronics industry, especially the advent of 5G networks, the popularity of electronic appliances and radio communications have increased in recent
years. However, despite the communication facilitated by modern electronic devices, the accompanying electromagnetic pollution has gradually attracted people's attention [1]. Massive electromagnetic waves (EWs) generated by the widespread use of electromagnetic equipment adversely affect the living environment, military, and commercial applications [2]. Accordingly, there is an urgent need to explore effective strategies to develop materials with high-performance electromagnetic wave absorption (EWA), strong
adsorption, wide effective absorption bandwidth, and good impedance matching, which can alleviate the current electromagnetic pollution.
It is well known that the EWA of the material depends on the loss mechanism and impedance matching, which is also affected by the material microstructure [3]. Therefore, exploring high-performance EWA materials with distinct microstructures to improve the EWA capacity has become a research hotspot [4]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), are a class of three-dimensional (3D) porous ordered crystalline materials formed by self-assembly of inorganic metal ions or metal clusters and organic ligands [5]. MOFs exhibit superior characteristics of high porosity, low density, large specific surface, and tunable structures [6], and have been widely used in applications such as batteries, supercapacitors, catalysis, and sensing [7]. In light of the multiple attenuation mechanisms, MOFs with self-assembled hierarchical structures and composed of metal (cluster) centers and organic bridging ligands can be considered as promising absorbents for EWs [8]. Generally, materials with high-performance EWA possess relatively good complex permittivity and permeability, which determine the storage and loss abilities for EW energy [9]. The EWA properties of materials are largely influenced by the synergy between components, dimensions, electrical conductivity, and modulated electrical and magnetic features. The optimization of electromagnetic parameters is facilitated by rationally controlling the heat-treatment process of MOFs [4,10]. The resulting MOF-derived carbon composites have been used as efficient electromagnetic absorbers. For example, the nanoporous \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{carbon}\) \((\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C})\) composites derived from Fe-MOFs were reported by Zhen et al. using simple one-pot method and subsequent pyrolysis [11]. Optimized impedance matching facilitated the absorption and attenuation of EWs. Liu et al. fabricated the highly porous nickel (Ni)-based MOF-derived Ni/C composites at a temperature of \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [12]. A minimum reflection loss \((R_{\mathrm{min}})\) of \(-51.8\) dB and an effective absorption bandwidth of \(3.48\:\mathrm{GHz}\) were achieved.
Currently, many studies have focused on monometallic MOF-derived composites for EWA. However, the band hybridization between the bimetallic elements may affect the electrical and magnetic properties of the resulting materials. Therefore, the construction of bimetallic MOF-derived composites will provide a new inspiration for designing materials with more effective EWA [13]. More importantly, the impedance matching and EWA of the heterogeneous bimetallic MOFs and their derivative carbon materials with different topologies can be facilitated by the presence of the metallic centers with different electron configurations and highly coordinated electrical and magnetic elements compared with conventional monometallic MOFs [14]. To the best of our knowledge, the ratios of metal ions and organic ligands of bimetallic MOFs, reaction temperature and time, as well as subsequent conditions of pyrolysis may change the morphology, structure, and electromagnetic properties of the obtained carbon composites, thereby affecting the EWA performances of the materials. Overall, the high-performance composites derived from bimetallic MOFs with magnetic metal and carbon elements may be considered as more efficient EWA materials [2].
In this work, electrically conductive and magnetic cobalt (Co)- \(\mathrm{Ni}@C\) composites with flower-like structures derived from CoNi bimetallic MOFs were fabricated using solvothermal method, followed by heat treatment. The morphology and microstructures of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites were modulated by altering the ratios between bimetallic ions and organic ligands. The boundary conditions for material formation were established by determining the mechanism of structural changes in bimetallic CoNi-MOFs, which facilitated the controllable preparation of porous CoNi@C materials with highly efficient EWA.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\) was purchased from Saen Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. (China). Nickel nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) was obtained from Jinhua Southeast Chemical Instrument Co., Ltd. (China). Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, PBO) was supplied by Nanjing Herbal Source Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (China). 1,4-phthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), and ethanol were ordered from Shanghai Merrill Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. (China). The deionized water used in the experiment was generated in our laboratory. All chemicals were of analytical grade without any other purification step involved.
## 2.2. Preparation of CoNi@C composites
The CoNi-MOFs were synthesized via a facial solvothermal method. \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) , PVP \(_{(1.2\mathrm{~g~})}\) , and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) \(_{(0.6645\mathrm{~g~})}\) were added to the solution \(\left(50\mathrm{~mL}\right)\) consisting of DMF and ethanol in a volume ratio of 3:2 and stirred using a magnetic stirrer (MS-300, Shanghai BANTE Instrument Co., Ltd., China) to obtain a homogeneous and transparent mixture. Then, it was transferred to a Teflon lined reaction kettle at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The CoNi-MOF products were repeatedly washed with deionized water and ethanol three times until neutral, followed by centrifugation (TDL-5-A, Shanghai Anting Scientific Instrument Factory, China) at a speed of \(3000~\mathrm{r/min}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) , and dried completely in an oven (DZF-6020, Shanghai Yiheng Scientific Instrument Co., Ltd., China) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{5\mathrm{~h~}}\) . Next, the CoNi-MOFs were annealed in a tubular furnace (SKGL-1200, Shanghai Duheng Optical Precision Machinery Co., Ltd., China) at \(600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{2\mathrm{~h~}}\) under Ar flow at a heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) , resulting in \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites (Fig. 1a). The coordination existed between the metal element and inorganic linker, with a coordination diversity between organic and inorganic units. The specific structure was regulated by changing the ratios between bimetallic ions and organic ligands [15]. In order to investigate the effects of different ratios of the \(\mathsf{C o}/\) Ni additions on the microstructures and electromagnetic properties of the obtained \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites, the molar ratios of Co \((\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were set to 0:1, 1:1, 1:2, 2:1, and 1:0, which were denoted as \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) , \(\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\) , and \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C}\) , respectively.
## 2.3. Characterizations
The micro-morphological structures and elemental composition and distribution of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites were observed using a scanning electron microscope (SEM, EM30-PLUS, COSEM) equipped with an energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS, Oxford, UK). The crystal structures of the samples were characterized by an X-ray diffractometer (XRD, D8 Advance, Bruker, Germany). The Raman data were recorded with a Raman spectrometer (RM1000, Renishaw, UK). The specific surface area and pore size distribution of the sample were measured using a \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption meter (ASAP 2460, Micromeritics, USA). A vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM, 7404, Lakeshore, USA) was used to test the static magnetisms of the samples based on the hysteresis loops obtained. The surface chemical composition of the sample was analyzed using an X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS, ESCALAB 250Xi, Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA). A vector network analyzer (VNA, ZVB20, Rohde & Schwarz, Germany) was used to analyze the EWA parameters of the samples in the frequency range of 2–18 GHz. The test samples including 30 wt% CoNi@C compos
ites and \(70\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) paraffin wax were pressed into a coaxial ring with an outer diameter of \(7\;\mathrm{mm}\) and an inner diameter of \(3.04\;\mathrm{mm}\).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure
The morphology and microstructures of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites synthesized with different \(\mathsf{C o/N i}\) molar ratios were shown in Fig. 1 and Fig. S1. The self-assembly of \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites was roughly three-phased: spheroid-petaoid spheroid. This structural change was attributed to the binding sequence of the metal ions and organic ligands, and the strength of coordination between different chemical bonds. Monometallic \(\mathsf{C o}_{0}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\) (Fig. S1a) and \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C}\) (Fig. S1d) exhibited their own unique growth directions structurally because of the strong coordination bonds between monometallic ions and organic ligands, which determined the spherical structures (with a particle size of \(3{-}4~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) and \(4{-}5~\upmu\mathrm{m}\), respectively) of the resulting MOF derivatives. The 3D flower-like structures with a particle size of \({\sim}2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) formed by stacking layered petals were self-assembled in \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\) by manipulating Co and Ni ions with a \(1{:}2\,\mathsf{M}\) ratio (Fig. 1b). The chemical bonding between Co and Ni led to the formation of structural chains while changing the precursor ratios, and the Co and Ni nuclei were interlocked to result in the two-dimensional (2D) nanolayered structures with an approximate interlayer distance of \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) of the single petal (Fig. 1c and d). In addition, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) and PVP acted as stabilizer and dispersant, respectively, during the hydrothermal process [16,17], so that the regulated \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites were uniformly dispersed without obvious agglomeration. In order to minimize the surface energy under high-temperature heat treatment [18],
the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites were also spontaneously connected with each other to form clusters [19], which facilitated the self-assembly of porous 3D flower-like architectures. The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) further confirmed that the CoNi alloy nanoparticles were coated with graphitic carbon showing a \(0.34\:\mathrm{nm}\) interlayer distance, corresponding to the (002) crystal plane. It was attributed to the catalytic graphitization of metal particles under high temperature [20]. The interplanar spacings of \(0.18\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.20\,\mathrm{nm}\) referred to the (200) and (111) planes of the CoNi alloy, respectively (Fig. 1e). The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\) showed the characteristic diffraction rings of (220), (200), and (111) crystal planes of the CoNi alloys, and (002) crystal plane of graphitic carbon (Fig. 1f) [21,22], which confirmed the HRTEM results. Further, the specific surface area and pore size distribution of the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\) composite were investigated, as shown in Fig. 1g. Due to the existence of the porous structure inside the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\), the adsorption and desorption curves formed an obvious hysteresis loop, which was a typical type IV isotherm. The specific surface area was \(114.05\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\). A high pore volume of \(0.396\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) suggested the presence of a mesoporous structure. The peak formed at the center of \({\sim}24.56\ \mathrm{nm}\) further confirmed the formation of numerous mesopores. Based on Maxwell's garnet theory, the porous structure effectively attenuated the effective permittivity, which facilitated impedance matching [23]. Therefore, such a unique structural feature with high specific surface area and the presence of a large number of mesopores provided an abundance of porous interfaces and active sites for interface polarization and scattering to facilitate multiple reflections and scattering of incident EWs, which might effectively enhance the attenuation and absorption of EWs [24]. The element mappings (Fig. S2) revealed
the uniform distribution of Co, Ni, and C elements in the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites. The atomic ratio of Co and Ni was close to 1:2 (Fig. S2e), demonstrating effective introduction of the two metallic elements inside the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composites.
The crystal structures of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites were analyzed as shown in Fig. 2a. All samples showed three strong diffraction peaks located at \(44.3^{\circ}\), \(51.5^{\circ}\), and \(76.1^{\circ}\), corresponding to the (111), (200), and (220) crystal planes of the CoNi alloys, respectively [25]. Compared with \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{3}@\mathsf{C}\) and \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C}\), which carbonized from monometallic Ni-MOFs (JCPDS 04-0850) and Co-MOFs (JCPDS 15-0806) [26], the peaks of \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\), \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\), and \(\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\) exhibited a very slight shift, suggesting minor changes in lattice diffraction and formation of CoNi alloys [27]. The changes in the characteristic diffraction peak intensities of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites with various proportions demonstrated differences in crystallinity, which were related to the average crystallite size and the competition between Ni-substituted Co and Co-substituted Ni induced by phase transition [28]. Besides, a weak peak corresponding to the (002) crystal plane of graphitic carbon (JCPDS 41-1487) was observed at \(\sim25.3^{\circ}\), but it was largely masked by the CoNi alloy peak [29]. Notably, the absence of any other impurity peaks indicated the high purity of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites. The Raman spectra were used to evaluate the degree of graphitization of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites (Fig. 2b). Particularly, the D-band peak \((1350\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) and G-band peak \((1590\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) represented the existence of lattice defects or disordered carbon in \(\mathbf{sp}^{3}\) carbon atoms and the in-plane stretching vibrations or ordered carbon in \(\mathbf{sp}^{2}\) carbon atoms [25,30], respectively. The \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) was the ratio of the D-to-G peak intensities. Therefore, when the \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) ratio was less than 1, the larger the intensity ratio, the larger the lattice defects of C atoms, and the greater the disorder of the material [31]. The corresponding \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) values of \(\mathsf{C o}_{0}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\), \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\), \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\), \(\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C}\), and \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C}\) were 0.96, 0.92, 0.97, 0.95, and 0.94, respectively. The \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composite exhibited the largest intensity ratio due to the stronger catalytic ability of the CoNi alloy during the annealing process, indicating additional defects and disordered carbon inside the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composite compared with other samples [32]. Additional defects in the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composite could be considered as polarization centers [33], which were conducive to more dipole polarization and interface polarization and led to an increase in polarization loss, thereby enhancing the EW dissipation ability of the material [34].
