In order to further delineate the surface composition and chemical state of the sample, the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composite was subjected to XPS analysis. Based on the full XPS spectrum (Fig. 2c), the four main peaks of Ni 2p, Co 2p, O 1s, and C 1s were detected, corresponding to the binding energies of \(862\,\mathrm{eV}\), \(781\,\mathrm{eV}\), \(532\,\mathrm{eV}\), and \(284.4\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. Fig. 2d showed three binding peaks attributed to C−C, C−O−C, and O-C=O at \(284.5\,\mathrm{eV}\), \(285.1\,\mathrm{eV}\), and \(289\,\mathrm{eV}\) [35], respectively, indicating defects in the graphite layer and oxygen-containing groups in the \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C}\) composite due to the different electronegativity of carbon and oxygen atoms [21,36]. The split peaks of the Co 2p (Fig. 2e) indicated two valence states of \(\mathsf{C o}^{0}\) and \(\mathsf{C o}^{2+}\) in the CoNi alloy. The binding energies of \(\mathsf{C o}^{0}\) were \(778.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(793.4\,\mathrm{eV}\), while the binding energies of satellite characteristic peaks of Co were \(785.7\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(802.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) [37], respectively. Similarly, a good fit of the two spin-orbit doublets in Ni 2p was observed (Fig. 2f), which was also attributed to the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) valence states of Ni in the CoNi alloy. The main peaks at \(852.6\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(869.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) corresponded to the spin-orbit splitting of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), and the two peaks near \(858.1\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(877.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) belonged to the oscillations of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) satellite, respectively. The main peaks at \(856.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(874.1\,\mathrm{eV}\) were attributed to the spin-orbit splitting of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) with the satellite characteristic peaks near \(862.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(881.0\,\mathrm{eV}\) [38]. Based on the foregoing analysis, except for some small oxygen peaks in the samples that might be attributed to slight oxidation in air after annealing, the pristine CoNi-MOFs were transformed into four species including \(\mathsf{C o}^{0}\), \(\mathsf{C o}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) chemical states of the MOF-derived carbon composites [39]. Further, the hysteresis loops of the \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites at room temperature were investigated, as shown in Fig. S3, which could be used to assess the static magnetism of the samples. Saturation magnetization \((M_{s})\) and coercivity \((H_{c})\) were important factors that determined the magnetic properties of materials, corresponding to the maximum storage capacity of the material for microwave energy based on the magnetic
component, and the hysteresis and permeability of the sample, respectively. The \( M_{s} \) and \( H_{c} \) values of \( \mathsf{C o}_{0}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C} \), \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C} \), \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C} \), \( \mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C} \), and \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C} \) were \( 32.1\,\mathrm{\emu/g} \) and \( 21.6\,\mathrm{\Oe} \), \( 81.2\,\mathrm{\emu/g} \) and \( 235\,\mathrm{\Oe} \), \( 49.7\,\mathrm{\emu/g} \) and \( 48.9\,\mathrm{\Oe} \), \( 98.4\,\mathrm{\emu/g} \) and \( 292.8\,\mathrm{\Oe} \), as well as \( 117.7\,\mathrm{\emu/g} \) and \( 447.4\,\mathrm{\Oe} \), respectively. The non-magnetic carbon layers existed on the surface of the pyrolyzed \( \mathsf{C o N i@C} \) composites, resulting in a relatively low \( M_{s} \). The relatively high \( H_{c} \) was ascribed to the increased surface anisotropy field due to the small particle size effect [40]. A high \( H_{c} \) contributed to the absorption of high-frequency EWs by the material [41].
## 3.2. EWA properties of \( \mathsf{C o N i@C} \) composites
The EWA performances of the \( \mathsf{C o N i@C} \) composites were reflected by the \( R L \) values of the EWs, which in turn were determined by the complex permittivity \( \left(\varepsilon_{r}\right) \) and complex permeability \( \left(\mu_{r}\right) \) according to the following formulas [42]:
\[
Z_{in}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}\operatorname{tanh}\left[j\frac{2\pi fd}{c}\sqrt{\mu_{r}/\varepsilon_{r}}\right]
\]
\[
RL(\mathrm{dB})=20\log\left|\frac{Z_{in}-Z_0}{Z_{in}+Z_0}\right|
\]
where \( Z_{i n},\,Z_{o},\,f,\,d, \) and c represented the input impedance of the absorbing material, the impedance of free space, the frequency of the incident EWs, the absorbing material thickness, and the propagation velocity of the EWs in vacuum, respectively. Generally, an \( R L \) value less than \( -10 \) dB suggested that \( 90\% \) of the microwaves were absorbed by the material. The corresponding absorption frequency range under this \( R L \) value was also known as the effective absorption bandwidth.
Fig. 3 showed the simulated \( R L \) curves, 3D projections, and 3D surface plots of the \( \mathsf{C o N i@C} \) composites with different thicknesses and ratios in the frequency range of \( 2{-}18\ \mathrm{GHz} \). In case of the monometallic MOF-derived \( \mathsf{C o}_{0}\mathsf{N i}_{1}@\mathsf{C} \) (Fig. 3a-c) and \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{0}@\mathsf{C} \) (Fig. 3m-o) composites, the limited \( R L_{min} \) values of \( -32.66 \) dB and \( -11.69 \) dB were obtained at a matching thickness of \( 3\ \mathrm{mm} \) and \( 5\ \mathrm{mm} \) with the effective absorption bandwidth of \( 3.94\ \mathrm{GHz} \) and \( 1.22\ \mathrm{GHz} \), respectively. When the molar ratio of \( \mathsf{C o} \) and Ni was 1:2, the \( \mathsf{C o N i@C} \) composite exhibited the best \( R L_{min} \) value of \( -56.89 \) dB at \( 6.7\ \mathrm{GHz} \) with an effective absorption bandwidth of \( 4.7\ \mathrm{GHz} \) (Fig. 3g-i), which was also superior to that of previously reported materials (e.g., 1-Ni/C nanorods [43], 2-C/Co [44], 3-CoNi@N-doped graphene [45], 4-N-doped Co-C/carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [46], 5-Ni/NiO/C [47], 6-Fe/C [48], 7-Ni/C [12], 8-Ni@SnO2 [49], 9-Co/N-doped carbon [50], 10-Co/CNTs nanohybrids [51], 11-Mo2C/C [52], 12-FeNi/C [53], 13-NiFe2S4/C [54], 14-CoFe@C [55], 15-Co/C@V2O3 [56]) (Fig. 4 and Table 1). This might be attributed to the well-coordinated dielectric effect and magnetic loss and 3D self-assembled flower-like structures interacting with microwaves, which effectively enhanced the attenuation and absorption of EWs.
The degree of impedance matching was a key factor determining the effectiveness of the material to absorb the incident EWs. Generally, a value of \( |Z_{i n}|/Z_{0}| \) closer to 1 resulted in better impedance matching of the material [12]. In this study, the EWs entered the interior of the material more easily, and the ability to absorb the EWs could be improved. Obviously, the \( |Z_{i n}|/Z_{0}| \) of the \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C} \) was closest to 1 in Fig. 5a. Therefore, compared with other samples, it exhibited better EWA properties. To illustrate the excellent EWA properties of the \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C} \) composites, the fitting curve \( (t_{m}^{s i m}) \) of the \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C} \) matching thickness and frequency, and the \( t_{m}^{e x p} \) of the thicknesses corresponding to different \( R L \) curves were further described (Fig. 5b). According to the \( 1/4 \) wavelength cancellation equation, the optimal \( R L \) value shifted to low frequencies with increasing thicknesses [57], which could be represented by the following \( 1/4 \) wavelength matching model [58]: \( t_{m}=\frac{n_{\lambda}^{2}}{4}=\frac{n c}{4\mu_{m}\sqrt{|\varepsilon_{r}|\mu_{r}|}}(n=1,3,5\cdots)(3) \) where \( t_{m} \) and \( \lambda \) represented the matching thickness at \( R L_{min} \) and wavelength, respectively. When the \( f_{m} \) and \( t_{m} \) satisfied the above formula, the two reflected EWs with phase differences of \( 180^{\circ} \) were cancelled at the interfaces between the air and absorbing material. The results show that the relationship between thickness and frequency of the \( \mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}@\mathsf{C} \) composites was basically consistent with the matching model, indicating the inverse relationship between \( f_{m} \) and \( t_{m} \).
