current loss was the dominant magnetic loss in this frequency range. Fig. 6e and f represented variations in dielectric loss tangent (tan \(\delta_{\varepsilon}\) ) and magnetic loss tangent (tan \(\delta_{\mu}\) ) at different frequencies, respectively. The tan \(\delta_{\mu}\) values were greater than those of tan \(\delta_{\varepsilon}\) in
the frequency range of \(6{-}10~\mathrm{GHz}\) , suggesting the major role of dielectric loss in this range. In the frequency range of \(12{-}15~\mathrm{GHz}\) the tan \(\delta_{\varepsilon}\) values were higher than tan \(\delta_{\mu}\) , and thus the primary contributor to magnetic loss. In general, the absorption of EWs
was actually facilitated by the synergistic effects of dielectric and magnetic loss. Moreover, the EWA performances of the material also depended on the attenuation coefficient \((\alpha)\), which was an indispensable requirement [67] and was usually used to evaluate the EW attenuation, according to formula S3 [68]. As shown in Fig. S5b, the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Co}_{1}\mathrm{Ni}_{2}@\mathrm{C}\) composite exhibited the largest \(\alpha\) value, indicating excellent microwave attenuation, which was attributed to the well-coordinated dielectric effect and 3D flower-like structure for effectively improving the attenuation of EWs.
Further, the multiple EWA mechanisms of the \(\mathrm{CoNi}@\mathrm{C}\) composites were depicted in Fig. 7 for clarity. First, when the external EWs contacted the front surface of the composite, only a very small component was reflected due to the coordinated impedance matching at air-absorber interfaces and porous flower-like structures [24], and most of them entered the interior of the material. The incident EWs were reflected and scattered multiple times inside the 3D porous flower-like structures with high specific sur
face area formed by multiple layers of interlaced 2D petal-shaped nanosheets. Second, the carbonized \(\mathsf{C o N i@C}\) composites with an effective complementarity between magnetic and dielectric losses carried a large number of pores and heterogeneous interfaces among CoNi alloys and graphitic carbon layers, which provided
numerous active sites and charge accumulations, resulting in strong multiple interface polarizations correlated with relaxations for the absorption and exhaustion of EWs [4,69]. Multiple defects in graphite carbon layers and oxygen-containing groups as polarization centers could lead to the enhanced dipole polarization
[70]. In addition, the composites with natural resonance and eddy current losses also played an important role in the attenuation of EWs [71,72]. Finally, the interspace existing in the flower-like structures generated microwave plasma [73], which converted EW energy into heat or other forms of energy [49]. In general, the flower-like CoNi@carbon composites with tunable structures, which were obtained by adjusting the ratios between bimetallic ions and organic ligands, could exhibit high \( RL_{min} \) value and wide effective absorption bandwidth and represent excellent and promising microwave-absorbing materials.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the flower-like CoNi@C composites exhibiting efficient EWA were successfully synthesized from bimetallic MOFs using solvothermal method and high-temperature heat treatment. This provided a feasible scheme for making up for the deficiency of single composition for conventional monometallic MOFs [6] and resulting in highly coordinated electrical and magnetic elements for more effective EWA [74]. More importantly, by controlling the ratios of bimetallic ions and organic ligands, the structures of the CoNi@C composites could be fine-tuned due to the coordination diversity between organic and inorganic units [75]. Particularly, the flower-like \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\) composite was obtained when the Co-Ni molar ratio was 1:2. The \(\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{@C}\) composite exhibited a superior \(R L_{m i n}\) value of \(-56.89\) dB at \(6.7\:\mathrm{GHz}\) and a wide effective absorption bandwidth of \(4.7\ \mathrm{\GHz}\) , which was significantly enhanced compared with other samples and some previously reported absorbers [12,50,52]. This was attributed to a combination of appropriate dielectric and magnetic losses, well impedance matching, and 3D porous flower-like structures of heterogeneous composites for interface polarizations, which synergistically enhanced the attenuation and absorption of EWs [76]. The MOF-derived CoNi@C composites with unique nano/micro-structures can be used to develop high-performance EW absorbers for effectively alleviating electromagnetic pollution, which is of great significance in the field of electromagnetic protection. Further, this simple and efficient structural control strategy will provide a proof of concept for fabricating other advanced functional composites.
